[0.0, "o", "Investigating REAL data for tenant \u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588: which acquisition source produces deals that actually close, not just deals that get created?\r\n\u001b[2m2026-09-22 20:02:13\u001b[0m [\u001b[33m\u001b[1mwarning  \u001b[0m] \u001b[1mmcp.nothing_admissible        \u001b[0m \u001b[36mlabel\u001b[0m=\u001b[35mposthog\u001b[0m \u001b[36mreason\u001b[0m=\u001b[35m'the server was queried and offered no tool Cortex can admit; every tool must declare readOnlyHint: true'\u001b[0m\r\nRead-only: no write capability exists in the tool registry.\r\nCapabilities available to \u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588: github, hubspot, posthog, slack\r\n  [  0.1s]          checking what data exists\r\n  [  1.4s]          found github__list_repositories\r\n  [  1.4s]          found posthog__list_events\r\n  [  1.4s]          found posthog__list_projects\r\n  [  1.4s]          checking memory for prior context\r\n  [  5.5s] step 1   deciding what to look at\r\n  [ 11.4s] step 1   calling hubspot__closed_won\r\n  [ 11.4s] step 1   calling hubspot__search\r\n  [ 16.2s] step 1   got hubspot__closed_won (4 observation(s) so far)\r\n  [ 16.2s] step 1   got hubspot__search (5 observation(s) so far)\r\n  [ 16.2s] step 2   deciding what to look at\r\n  [ 22.4s] step 2   calling hubspot__search\r\n  [ 22.4s] step 2   calling hubspot__search\r\n  [ 22.4s] step 2   got read hubspot.search in full (100 rows) (5 observation(s) so far)\r\n  [ 24.0s] step 2   got hubspot__search (6 observation(s) so far)\r\n  [ 24.0s] step 2   got hubspot__search (7 observation(s) so far)\r\n  [ 24.0s] step 3   deciding what to look at\r\n  [ 41.3s] step 3   calling hubspot__search\r\n  [ 43.0s] step 3   got hubspot__search (8 observation(s) so far)\r\n  [ 43.0s] step 4   deciding what to look at\r\n  [ 70.5s]          drafting the report from 8 observation(s)\r\n  [ 92.3s]          checking every citation resolves\r\n  [ 92.4s]          re-reading each claim against its own evidence\r\n  sufficiency gate: the evidence does NOT support a cause (6.4s); missing: Per-source breakdown of created deals vs closed-won deals (win rate by source) rather than aggregate lists of individual deals; A comparable time-matched cohort analysis showing conversion rate by acquisition source (e.g., OFFLINE vs ORGANIC_SEARCH vs INTEGRATION) rather than raw deal-level records; Aggregated counts/amounts \u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588ed by source field across the full closed-won and closed-lost populations, not just the first page of truncated records\r\n  sufficiency gate withheld 1 causal claim(s)\r\n  [112.5s]          writing the report\r\n\r\n==============================================================================\r\nQ: Which acquisition source produces deals that actually close, not just deals that get created?\r\n==============================================================================\r\n\r\nConfidence: insufficient evidence\r\nTook 92s over 4 steps, 51,529 tokens, 8 observations gathered, 5 cited\r\n\r\nANSWER\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  The evidence cannot reliably answer which acquisition source converts best: only \u2588 deals in the primary \u2588-month cohort have actually closed won (one OFFLINE, one ORGANIC_SEARCH), which is too few to distinguish source performance from noise.  [hubspot.search b12f696c]\r\n\r\nRISKS AND CAVEATS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - Only \u2588 closed-won deals exist in the primary \u2588-month creation window, which is too small a sample to compute or compare a win rate by source with any confidence.\r\n  - Two hubspot.search observations (\u2588\u2588 and \u2588\u2588\u2588 rows) were retrieved only as digests and not read row-by-row; conclusions here rely on the aggregate counts in those digests, not on inspecting individual deals within them.\r\n  - A \u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588 fraction of deals (\u2588-\u2588 per pull) have null source/source_detail, meaning true source-level close rates cannot be fully reconstructed from this data.\r\n  - \u2588 claim(s) were removed because an independent check found the cited evidence did not support them. Treat the remaining conclusions with corresponding caution.\r\n  - \u2588 claim(s) go beyond their evidence and are retained with reduced confidence rather than removed, because the rest of each one holds. What the check found:\r\n- findings[0].claims[0]: asked as an open question, with this claim withheld, the same evidence does not establish a cause: The data only shows two closed-won deals in the period (\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588 via OFFLINE/INTEG\r\n  - \u2588 causal claim(s) were withheld: a separate check of the evidence gathered, made without seeing this report, found that it does not support a definitive answer about cause. The observations list individual won and lost deals with source tags but never aggregate or compare close rates by source; a reader would have to manually tally and infer which source converts best, which the data as given does not do (and some lists are truncated), so no supported answer about which source 'actually closes' can be read off directly. What would settle it: Per-source breakdown of created deals vs closed-won deals (win rate by source) rather than aggregate lists of individual deals; A comparable time-matched cohort analysis showing conversion rate by acquisition source (e.g., OFFLINE vs ORGANIC_SEARCH vs INTEGRATION) rather than raw deal-level records; Aggregated counts/amounts \u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588ed by source field across the full closed-won and closed-lost populations, not just the first page of truncated records.\r\n\r\nDATA QUALITY\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - Closed-won deals are extremely rare in the sampled windows (\u2588 in the \u2588-month creation cohort, \u2588\u2588 in a broader closed-date pull with many nulls), so any source-level win-rate comparison is statistically unreliable and should be treated as directional at best.\r\n  - The github commits sync last succeeded \u2588\u2588 days ago. Stored history for it is that old, and a recent change may not appear.\r\n  - The github deployments sync last succeeded \u2588\u2588 days ago. Stored history for it is that old, and a recent change may not appear.\r\n  - The github issues sync last succeeded \u2588\u2588 days ago. Stored history for it is that old, and a recent change may not appear.\r\n  - The github links sync last succeeded \u2588\u2588 days ago. Stored history for it is that old, and a recent change may not appear.\r\n  - The posthog annotations sync last succeeded \u2588\u2588 days ago. Stored history for it is that old, and a recent change may not appear.\r\n  - The posthog events sync last succeeded \u2588\u2588 days ago. Stored history for it is that old, and a recent change may not appear.\r\n\r\nSOURCES\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  4fd439fb  hubspot.closed_won  hubspot://crm/v3/objects/deals/search?closed_won=2026-03-22..2026-09-22\r\n  127b7d00  hubspot.search  hubspot://crm/v3/objects/deals/search?createdate=2026-03-22..2026-09-22\r\n  567640fe  hubspot.search  hubspot://crm/v3/objects/deals/search?createdate=2026-03-22..2026-09-22&hs_is_closed_lost=true\r\n  b12f696c  hubspot.search  hubspot://crm/v3/objects/deals/search?createdate=2026-03-22..2026-09-22&hs_is_closed_won=true\r\n  db257b85  hubspot.search  hubspot://crm/v3/objects/deals/search?closedate=2026-03-22..2026-09-22&hs_is_closed_lost=true\r\n\r\nphase breakdown (112s wall, 110s measured)\r\n  phase        calls  seconds  share    mean  tokens\r\n  survey           1      1.4    1%     1.4  in 0 out 0\r\n  recall           1      4.1    4%     4.1  in 0 out 0\r\n  loop_model       4     56.9   51%    14.2  in 8 out 